  "compatibility": [
    "WebGL2 is required for the live sculpture. An original static relief remains available without JavaScript or graphics support.",
    "Captured pointer and touch input, keyboard contact and rotation controls, and HTML alternatives. Touch gestures are reserved inside the preview; the surrounding document scrolls normally.",
    "Reduced motion snaps pin targets and freezes automatic waves; explicit contact, rotation and phase inspection remain available. Motion pauses offscreen, in hidden tabs and through the Lab controls.",
    "32–128 columns, approximately 672–10,880 pins. Each pin is one instanced rod/head mesh with a single height attribute; no per-pin DOM elements. Resolution and shadows are capped."
  ],
  "notes": [
    "The rod itself remains rigid and keeps its length. Only Z translation changes. Travel limits keep both ends engaged with the guide board, with visible clearance at the front.",
    "Sphere, rounded cube, torus and star stamps use procedural height profiles. Pointer presses interpolate along fast movements; held strokes accumulate and temporary contact springs back.",
    "The original palm is a stylized procedural height study, not a face scan or anatomical reconstruction. Imported images supply brightness-based heights; no depth inference or upload occurs.",
    "A critically damped response settles each pin to its own target. Spatial smoothing blends neighboring target heights. Wave patterns update target heights, rather than moving a decorative texture.",
    "Physically lit materials, a small procedural studio environment, pin sockets, an engraved scale and a beveled frame make the board legible from an angled view. The moving-pin shadow material uses the same height displacement as the visible geometry."
  ],
  "reuse": [
    "The source ZIP includes the complete Three.js runtime and license, original assets, controls, model and a standalone example; no CDN or remote model is required.",
    "Use createPinField(canvas, settings, onFrame) for an independent engine. Feed procedural height functions, normalized height maps, pointer locations or serialized sculptures.",
    "model.js owns normalized heights, smoothing and dynamics; geometry.js owns rods and shader displacement; engine.js owns rendering; images.js performs local decoding; index.js is the optional Lab UI.",
    "README.md documents world coordinates, contact inputs, image mapping, limits, presets, import/export, lifecycle and integration. Source exports contain original studies and settings, not private images or visitor artwork."
  ]
}
